Mobile Internet Speed Analysis Across Locations is a data-driven project that evaluates the performance of 
mobile internet networks across different geographical locations. The system collects key network 
parameters such as download speed, upload speed, latency, signal strength, jitter, packet loss, and network 
type (4G/5G) using speed test applications and GPS-based location tracking. The collected data is stored in a 
centralized database and analyzed to identify variations in network performance, detect weak coverage 
areas, and compare internet quality across different regions. 
Statistical analysis and visualization techniques are used to generate graphs, charts, and location-based maps 
for easy interpretation of the results. The project also examines the impact of factors such as location, time 
of day, network congestion, and signal strength on internet performance. By analyzing trends over multiple 
observations, the system provides a more accurate assessment of network reliability rather than relying on a 
single speed test. 
Furthermore, the project enables comparison of different mobile network providers based on real-world 
performance metrics and identifies areas where users experience poor connectivity. The generated insights 
can help users select the most suitable network for a particular location while assisting telecom service 
providers in improving coverage, optimizing network resources, and enhancing overall Quality of Service 
(QoS). The proposed system offers a scalable and cost-effective approach for continuous mobile network 
performance monitoring and can be extended to support predictive analytics and smart city connectivity 
planning in the future.
